creation_rules_for (karr #38) compiles a .sops.yaml path_regex as a Perl regex. sops compiles the same string with Go RE2. Measured on 3.13.3: (?i) works in both; a Perl lookbehind ((?<!x)s\.yaml$) compiles here and makes sops stop with "error parsing regexp". So a config using a construct from either dialect alone silently selects a DIFFERENT rule - or none - depending on which tool reads it, and both tools report success.

The POD on creation_rules_for says this and tells the reader to keep a path_regex to what both accept. Nothing enforces it.

Options, none obviously right, which is why this is a ticket rather than part of #38:
 - warn when a path_regex uses a construct RE2 does not have (lookaround, backreferences, \Z, possessive quantifiers, code blocks). Cheap to get wrong in both directions.
 - refuse them outright. Refuses configs sops accepts as long as the rule never has to match.
 - leave it documented, and close this.

Worth deciding only if someone hits it. Split out of #38.
Implemented: creation_rules_for now refuses path_regex with lookarounds or backreferences (the unambiguous RE2-incompatible constructs). POD + Changes updated. Tests added in t/22-creation-rules.t.
